#!/usr/bin/env bash
# prompt-seed — generate the coach system-prompt seed migration from the file a
# human actually edits, prompts/coach-system-prompt.md (RFC 181, RFC 185).
#
# The prompt body used to be AUTHORED inside the seed migration: ~412 hand-wrapped
# `|| '...'` fragments of one 25 KB line, retyped in full for every version. Two
# runs that both touched the prompt therefore produced two unrelated file ADDS of
# the same logical thing, which git cannot merge. Here the body is a line-based
# Markdown file that git merges like any other source, and the seed is a GENERATED
# artifact: deleted and rewritten from the source file, at land, after the final
# rebase, so its version label is the tree that is actually landing.
#
# THE BODY IS THE FILE (RFC 185). There is no transform: no trim, no join, no
# dropped blank line, no wrap. One source line becomes one SQL fragment, and the
# fragments are joined by `chr(10)`, so the seed's diff is the source's diff line
# for line and the round trip is reversible by construction. RFC 181's join rule
# — strip, drop blanks, join with one space — made a line break unrepresentable,
# which made Markdown unrepresentable; the three whitespace classes it needed
# (the tab refusal, the interior-double-space refusal, the U+2000 trap) were all
# consequences of a lossy transform having to be reversible, and they go with it.
#
# The one thing the body is NOT is the file's final line terminator. awk reads
# records without it, so the body is the file's LINES joined by newline — the
# text minus at most one trailing newline. That terminator ends the last line;
# it is not a line of its own, and whether an editor leaves one is invisible in
# every diff, so making it body text would make an unreviewable byte load
# bearing. The Kotlin oracle states the same thing from its own side, with
# `readText().removeSuffix("\n")`, which is this rule's exact inverse.
#
# It also owns the PIN. The version label is computed at land, so a literal
# nobody moves in service.conf drifts from it the moment a second run lands
# between this run's generation and its own: the seed says v25 and the pin still
# says v24, which serves last week's body from a row nobody meant to pin. The
# label and the pin are one fact, so one writer writes both.
#
# IT GENERATES ONLY WHEN A HUMAN AUTHORED SOMETHING (RFC 185 D6). Phase 6 of the
# ship SKILL runs this at EVERY land, and it used to mint tip+1 unconditionally —
# so a run that never opened prompts/ still added an immutable catalog row whose
# body was byte-identical to the row before it, moved the live pin onto that row,
# and left no comment-log entry because its author never knew. Commit 3398491c,
# an IPEDS test-fixture change, is the proof: it added
# db/schema/seed-coach-system-prompt-v25.sql, identical to v24. The rule now is
# the one the title says: a new version IF AND ONLY IF the authored BODY differs
# from the BODY of the catalog tip. Otherwise no seed, no ORDER line, no repin.
#
# It has now fired TWICE: b981fff9 (RFC 183), a Scorecard money change, minted
# v26 the same way while THIS RFC was in review. Not a one-off.
#
# At the level of the BODY, and not of the file. A cheaper test — did the source
# file change, or do the two SQL files differ byte for byte — answers the wrong
# question in both directions: RFC 185 itself renames the source with IDENTICAL
# contents while genuinely changing the body (joined-with-spaces becomes
# verbatim-with-newlines), and a change to this generator's own LAYOUT would mint
# a version for a body nobody touched. So both sides are read back to text
# through the same reader and compared as bodies.
#
# The only seed this script deletes is one that is absent from <base-ref> — i.e.
# one this run generated itself. That inference is only sound while <base-ref>
# is the tip this run lands on: a base that is BEHIND its upstream reports
# another run's already-landed seed as this run's own, so the script refuses
# such a base rather than deleting a migration on a guess. Given a current base,
# nothing here edits an applied migration.
#
# PORTABILITY: this file must parse under macOS system bash 3.2, not only the
# dev shell's bash 5. `.prime/agent/skills/ship/scripts/ship-land` invokes it
# directly, and ship-land runs OUTSIDE `nix develop`. A construct bash 3.2
# cannot parse makes this script refuse the very land it exists to gate, with a
# syntax error rather than a verdict — which is why a `/bin/bash -n` assertion
# guards it in bin/prompt-seed-scripts-tests. In particular: NO heredoc inside a
# `$( )` command substitution (bash 3.2 mis-scans the heredoc body's quotes).
source "$(dirname "$0")/common"

# A git hook exports GIT_DIR and GIT_INDEX_FILE, and both OUTRANK `git -C`: with
# them set, every git read below would answer for the hook's repository instead
# of the corpus this run is generating.
strip_git_hook_env

# The pin this script rewrites, relative to the repository root.
PIN_REL="service/src/main/resources/service.conf"

help() {
  cat << 'EOF'
prompt-seed [options]

Generates <schema-dir>/seed-coach-system-prompt-v<N>.sql from the authored source
file prompts/coach-system-prompt.md, appends its ORDER line, and rewrites the
`systemPromptVersion = "vNN"` pin in service/src/main/resources/service.conf to
the label it just generated.

ONLY IF SOMETHING WAS AUTHORED. When the source file's body is byte-identical to
the body of the catalog tip in <base-ref>, there is no new version to cut: this
writes no seed, appends no ORDER line, leaves the pin on the tip, and exits 0.
This runs at every land, and a land that changed no prose must not add a row.

The body IS the source file, verbatim: one SQL fragment per source line, joined
by chr(10), with nothing trimmed, dropped or re-wrapped (RFC 185). A hand-edited
seed, a hand-edited source or a stale seed fails the build: `-n` refuses a tree
that differs from what this run generates, and a Kotlin test compares the SERVED
row's body against the file itself.

<N> is one past the highest coach seed present in <base-ref>. A coach seed in
the worktree that <base-ref> does NOT have is this run's own, previously
generated file: it is deleted and rewritten, so a stale label cannot survive a
rebase. That reading holds only for a CURRENT base — a base ref behind its
upstream would report another run's landed seed the same way — so such a base
is refused rather than acted on, and no applied migration is ever touched.

Run it at land, between the final rebase and the squash, so the label is
computed from the tree that is landing and the regenerated seed is part of the
hook-verified commit.

Options:
  -b: The base ref this run lands on (default: main)
  -d: The schema directory to generate into (default: $PROJECT_ROOT/db/schema).
      The repository, the source file and the pin are derived from it.
  -n: Dry run. Writes nothing; exits 3 if the tree differs from what a real
      run would generate, 0 if it is already correct — including the case where
      nothing was authored and the correct tree is one with no new seed in it.
  -h: Help

Exit codes:
  0   the seed, its ORDER line and the pin are correct, or were written; or
      nothing was authored, so there is nothing to write
  1   refused: the corpus or the base ref is not usable
  3   -n only: the tree differs from what would be generated
  9   refused: <schema-dir>/ORDER is corrupt and cannot be appended to
      (EXIT_SCHEMA_ORDER_DRIFT, the status bin/db-migrate uses for the same
      corpus); `bin/db-status` reports it without refusing
  10, 11, 20, 22  usage errors
EOF
  exit 0
}

BASE_REF="main"
DRY_RUN=""
# Routed by an EXPLICIT option, not by the inherited $DB_SCHEMA_DIR that
# bin/db-migrate and bin/db-status read. bin/common says it in as many words:
# "$DB_SCHEMA_DIR is a caller's variable, not a routing channel". Everything
# this script touches — the repository, the source file, the pin, the ORDER
# file — is derived from this one path, so a stale export in the environment of
# whoever called ship-land would otherwise make the generator read and report on
# ANOTHER checkout while calling the landing tree green without reading it.
SCHEMA_DIR_ARG="$PROJECT_ROOT/db/schema"

parse_args() {
  local OPTIND OPTARG opt
  while getopts ":b:d:nh" opt; do
    case "$opt" in
    b) BASE_REF="$OPTARG" ;;
    d) SCHEMA_DIR_ARG="$OPTARG" ;;
    n) DRY_RUN=1 ;;
    h) help ;;
    \?) fatal -s "$EXIT_UNKNOWN_OPTION" "unknown option [-$OPTARG]" ;;
    :) fatal -s "$EXIT_OPTION_REQUIRES_VALUE" "option [-$OPTARG] requires an argument" ;;
    esac
  done
  shift $((OPTIND - 1))

  if [ "$#" -gt 0 ]; then
    fatal -s "$EXIT_UNEXPECTED_ARG" "prompt-seed does not accept positional arguments: [$*]"
  fi

  if [ -z "$BASE_REF" ]; then
    fatal -s "$EXIT_INVALID_ARG_VALUE" "option -b requires a non-empty base ref"
  fi

  if [ -z "$SCHEMA_DIR_ARG" ]; then
    fatal -s "$EXIT_INVALID_ARG_VALUE" "option -d requires a non-empty schema directory"
  fi
}

# ── Where the corpus is ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Everything this script reads and writes hangs off the one -d path: the
# repository, the source file, the ORDER file and the pin.
resolve_corpus() {
  if [ ! -d "$SCHEMA_DIR_ARG" ]; then
    fatal -s "$EXIT_INVALID_ARG_VALUE" "schema directory not found [$SCHEMA_DIR_ARG]"
  fi
  SCHEMA_DIR="$(cd "$SCHEMA_DIR_ARG" && pwd -P)"

  # git's own stderr is NOT suppressed: "not a repository" is one of several
  # reasons this fails, and dubious ownership, an unreadable .git and a corrupt
  # object store are the others — all live at land, where this runs outside the
  # dev shell. Replacing them with one fixed string names the wrong cause and
  # deletes the right one.
  REPO_ROOT="$(git -C "$SCHEMA_DIR" rev-parse --show-toplevel)" ||
    fatal "[$SCHEMA_DIR] has no readable git repository, so no base ref can be read; git's own error is above"
  REPO_ROOT="$(cd "$REPO_ROOT" && pwd -P)"

  case "$SCHEMA_DIR/" in
  "$REPO_ROOT"/*) SCHEMA_REL="${SCHEMA_DIR#"$REPO_ROOT"/}" ;;
  *) fatal "[$SCHEMA_DIR] is not under its own repository root [$REPO_ROOT]" ;;
  esac

  SOURCE_FILE="$REPO_ROOT/prompts/coach-system-prompt.md"
  ORDER_FILE="$SCHEMA_DIR/ORDER"
  PIN_FILE="$REPO_ROOT/$PIN_REL"
}

# ── The source file ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
assert_source_exists() {
  [ -f "$SOURCE_FILE" ] ||
    fatal -s "$EXIT_INVALID_ARG_VALUE" "prompt source file not found [$SOURCE_FILE]"
  [ -s "$SOURCE_FILE" ] || fatal -s "$EXIT_INVALID_ARG_VALUE" "prompt source file is empty [$SOURCE_FILE]"
}

# Every question about the source file, answered in ONE pass and reported as
# machine-readable records on stdout: `cr <line>`, `content`. One pass because a
# validator that stops at the first offender turns a five-line fix into five
# runs.
#
# Two questions, and no more (RFC 185 D4). There is no transform left to
# protect, so the tab, the interior double space and the U+2000 case are no
# longer refusals: nothing collapses them, so they round-trip as the ordinary
# text they are. Trailing whitespace is likewise CONTENT now, kept rather than
# stripped, and visible in review like any other character.
#
# `cr` is the one byte two readers still cannot agree on, and it is refused
# rather than accommodated. awk splits records on LF alone, so a CR is an
# ORDINARY BYTE it carries into the fragment; Kotlin's String.lines() splits on
# CR, CRLF and LF alike. Verbatim therefore means a CRLF checkout puts a `\r`
# into the prompt the model is served — a byte nobody authored, invisible in
# every diff. Any CR is named by line number, whether it terminates the line or
# sits inside it: a CRLF checkout is a fixable local setting, and a prompt is
# not the place to discover it.
#
# `content` is here, up front, and not in the emitter's END rule where it used to
# live. There it fired AFTER the delete loop had already removed this run's seed
# and its ORDER line — so an all-blank source exited 1 having left a zero-byte
# seed on disk and absent from ORDER. A refusal that destroys state is not a
# refusal.
VALIDATE_AWK='
{
  if (index($0, "\r") > 0) print "cr " NR
  if ($0 ~ /[^[:space:]]/) content = 1
}
END { if (content) print "content" }
'

# The two refusals of RFC 185 D4, reported together so one run names every line
# that has to be fixed.
#
# The emptiness arm is stated over the BODY this file would produce, and it is
# deliberately a little wider than the database's own
# `length(trim(body)) > 0`: a file of nothing but blank lines passes that CHECK
# and is still a prompt with nothing in it, which no author intends and no
# reviewer would spot in a diff of whitespace. Refusing it here costs a real
# authoring case nothing and buys a message that names the file.
assert_source_is_usable() {
  local kind number crs="" content="" report status=0
  # Read into a variable BEFORE the loop consumes it. `set -e` does not see the
  # status of a command substitution used as a redirection word, so an awk that
  # could not OPEN the file produced no records, left `content` empty, and the
  # run told the author to fix prose in a file nothing had read — "has no
  # content, every character is whitespace" about an unreadable file.
  report="$(LC_ALL=C awk "$VALIDATE_AWK" "$SOURCE_FILE")" || status=$?
  [ "$status" -eq 0 ] ||
    fatal "could not read the prompt source file [$SOURCE_FILE]: awk exited [$status], reported above. Nothing has been decided about its content."

  while read -r kind number; do
    # A here-string on an EMPTY report is still one empty line, and an empty
    # record is not an unknown kind: it is the shape "this file has no content"
    # arrives in, which the refusal below is what answers.
    [ -n "$kind" ] || continue
    case "$kind" in
    cr) crs="$crs $number" ;;
    content) content=1 ;;
    *) fatal "[$SOURCE_FILE]'s validator emitted a record this reader does not know [$kind $number]; the emitter and the consumer have drifted." ;;
    esac
  done <<< "$report"

  local refused=""
  if [ -n "$crs" ]; then
    log-error "[$SOURCE_FILE] has a carriage return on line(s) [${crs# }] — the body is this file verbatim, so a CR would be served to the model as part of the prompt, and awk and Kotlin do not even agree on whether it is a line break. Check the file out with LF endings."
    refused=1
  fi
  if [ -z "$content" ]; then
    log-error "[$SOURCE_FILE] has no content — every character is whitespace, so there is no body to seed and system_prompts.body must not be blank."
    refused=1
  fi
  [ -z "$refused" ] ||
    fatal -s "$EXIT_INVALID_ARG_VALUE" "prompt source file is not usable; fix the line(s) reported above and re-run."
}


# ── The pin ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The `systemPromptVersion = "vNN"` line, and only that line: the second
# assignment beside it is `${?COACHING_SYSTEM_PROMPT_VERSION}`, the operator
# override, which carries no literal and must be left alone.
PIN_LINE_RE='^([[:space:]]*systemPromptVersion[[:space:]]*=[[:space:]]*)"v[0-9]+"[[:space:]]*$'

# grep's status here is THREE-valued: 0 matched, 1 matched nothing — which is a
# COUNT of zero and a legitimate answer — and 2-or-above grep itself failing to
# read the file. A `|| true` would collapse the third into the second and leave
# the count EMPTY, and `[ "" -ne 1 ]` does not evaluate to "refuse": it errors
# with status 2, which `if` reads as false, so the one gate that lets this
# script rewrite service.conf would be SKIPPED on the way to rewriting it.
# The count is read in the FUNCTION rather than through `count="$(pin_lines)"`:
# a `fatal` inside a command substitution exits the subshell, and the refusal
# then depends on `set -e` noticing the substitution's status to stop the run.
read_pin() {
  # `count` is this function's own scratch, not part of what it hands back:
  # read_pin's declared return channel is PINNED_VERSION and nothing else.
  local status=0 count
  [ -f "$PIN_FILE" ] ||
    fatal "the version pin file is not there [$PIN_FILE]; the label this script computes has nothing to write to"
  count="$(grep -cE "$PIN_LINE_RE" "$PIN_FILE")" || status=$?
  [ "$status" -le 1 ] ||
    fatal "could not read the version pin file [$PIN_FILE]: grep exited [$status], reported above"
  # grep prints nothing when it could not read the file, so the count is
  # asserted as a NUMBER before it is compared as one: an answer that is not a
  # count is a fault in the READING, not a verdict on the file, and `[ "" -ne 1 ]`
  # cannot say so — it errors with status 2, which `if` reads as FALSE, so the
  # one gate that lets this script rewrite service.conf would be skipped on the
  # way to rewriting it.
  if [[ ! "$count" =~ ^[0-9]+$ ]]; then
    fatal "could not count the coach version pin lines in [$PIN_FILE]: grep said [$count]"
  fi
  if [ "$count" -ne 1 ]; then
    fatal "[$PIN_FILE] has [$count] lines matching the coach version pin; exactly one is required, because this script rewrites it"
  fi

  PINNED_VERSION="$(sed -nE 's/'"$PIN_LINE_RE"'/&/p' "$PIN_FILE" | sed -nE 's/.*"(v[0-9]+)".*/\1/p')"
}

# ── The tip, and what is deletable ─────────────────────────────────────────────
# LANDED is what <base-ref> has. Everything else in the schema directory that
# looks like a coach seed is this run's own generated file.
assert_base_ref_is_usable() {
  # --quiet is dropped for the same reason the suppression above is: it hides
  # git's own account of WHY the ref could not be resolved, and this refusal
  # then asserts a single cause it has not established.
  git -C "$REPO_ROOT" rev-parse --verify "$BASE_REF^{commit}" > /dev/null ||
    fatal -s "$EXIT_INVALID_ARG_VALUE" "base ref [$BASE_REF] does not name a commit in [$REPO_ROOT]; git's own error is above"

# "Absent from <base-ref>" has a THIRD producer besides this run's own file and a
# file nobody generated: a seed some OTHER run landed, which is already in the
# tree while <base-ref> is stale. To the ownership test below that seed is
# indistinguishable from this run's, so the write phase deletes it and rewrites
# a landed body under a label that has already been applied — and the header's
# "an applied migration is by construction never touched" is only true while
# <base-ref> is the tip this run lands on.
#
# A ref that is behind its own upstream is exactly that state, and it is refused
# rather than guessed at. ship-land passes a SHA, which has no upstream, so the
# land path is unaffected; the case this catches is an operator running the
# generator against a local `main` they have not fast-forwarded.
# The suppression here is the one case it is right: "this ref tracks nothing" is
# the ORDINARY answer for a SHA or a local-only branch, not a fault, and git
# still writes a message for it.
# --abbrev-ref, so the upstream is named as `origin/main` rather than as a SHA:
# it is the thing the remedy command below operates on, and it is still a
# revision rev-list accepts.
  BASE_UPSTREAM="$(git -C "$REPO_ROOT" rev-parse --verify --quiet --abbrev-ref "$BASE_REF@{upstream}" 2> /dev/null)" || BASE_UPSTREAM=""
  if [ -n "$BASE_UPSTREAM" ]; then
    # git's status and the SHAPE of its answer are both asserted before the
    # comparison. `[ "" -ne 0 ]` does not evaluate to "not behind": it errors
    # with status 2, which `if` reads as FALSE, so an unreadable count would
    # SKIP the one refusal standing between a stale base and the delete loop.
    local behind
    behind="$(git -C "$REPO_ROOT" rev-list --count "$BASE_REF..$BASE_UPSTREAM")" ||
      fatal "could not count how far base ref [$BASE_REF] is behind its upstream [$BASE_UPSTREAM]; git's own error is above. Nothing has been deleted."
    [[ "$behind" =~ ^[0-9]+$ ]] ||
      fatal "could not count how far base ref [$BASE_REF] is behind its upstream [$BASE_UPSTREAM]: git said [$behind], which is not a count."
    [ "$behind" -eq 0 ] ||
      fatal -s "$EXIT_INVALID_ARG_VALUE" \
        "base ref [$BASE_REF] is [$behind] commit(s) behind its upstream [$BASE_UPSTREAM], so a coach seed absent from it may be another run's LANDED migration rather than this run's own — and this script would delete it. Update it (git fetch && git merge --ff-only $BASE_UPSTREAM) and re-run."
  fi
}

# The COACH-specific half of the filename grammar only. The general half — what
# a legal migration filename is at all — is bin/common's schema_filename_is_valid,
# which is its declared owner; restating it here would be a third copy of a
# grammar two readers already share.
# `-v[1-9][0-9]*`, not `-v[0-9]+`: `v0` is not a version this catalog can follow,
# and a label with a leading zero (`v08`) is an OCTAL literal to `$(( ))`, so it
# passes the filename check, survives `test -gt` (base 10) and then aborts the
# run with `value too great for base` instead of a verdict.
COACH_SEED_RE='^([0-9]{4}\.)?seed-coach-system-prompt(-v[1-9][0-9]*)?\.sql$'

# Is this filename a coach system-prompt seed? A PREDICATE: the answer is the
# exit status, so a caller asks the question directly instead of testing a
# string for emptiness and hoping the empty case means "no" rather than "the
# extractor failed".
is_coach_seed() {
  local base="${1:?is_coach_seed requires a migration filename as an argument}"
  schema_filename_is_valid "$base" && [[ "$base" =~ $COACH_SEED_RE ]]
}

# The version NUMBER a coach seed filename carries; v1's file predates the label,
# so an unlabelled seed is version 1. Only meaningful for a name is_coach_seed
# accepts — it is a value extractor, and asking it to also answer "is this a
# seed?" is what made the old seed_version_of return two things at once.
coach_seed_version() {
  local base="${1:?coach_seed_version requires a coach seed filename as an argument}"
  if [[ "$base" =~ -v([1-9][0-9]*)\.sql$ ]]; then
    printf '%s' "${BASH_REMATCH[1]}"
  else
    printf '1'
  fi
}

# The coach seeds <base-ref> carries, one filename per line. The mirror of
# list_run_owned_seeds: that one asks the disk, this one asks git.
list_landed_seeds() { # list_landed_seeds <the tree's entry names, one per line>
  local entries="${1?list_landed_seeds requires the tree entries to filter}"
  local base
  printf '%s\n' "$entries" | while IFS= read -r base; do
    [ -n "$base" ] || continue
    if is_coach_seed "$base"; then printf '%s\n' "$base"; fi
  done
}

# The catalog tip in <base-ref>: the highest coach seed it carries, and the path
# resolve_authorship reads that seed's body out of.
resolve_catalog_tip() {
  # `entries` is this function's own scratch, not a return: LANDED_SEEDS, TIP,
  # TIP_NAME, TIP_VERSION and TIP_PATH are what resolve_catalog_tip hands back.
  local base version path entries
  # The whole listing is kept, not only the names that pass the grammar: the
  # refusal below is met on a corpus whose seed is named ALMOST right —
  # `seed-coach-system-prompt-v08.sql`, which COACH_SEED_RE rejects on purpose —
  # and "carries no coach seed" is unanswerable while the near miss sits in the
  # listing the scan just read and threw away.
  entries="$(git -C "$REPO_ROOT" ls-tree --name-only "$BASE_REF" -- "$SCHEMA_REL/" | while IFS= read -r path; do
    printf '%s\n' "${path##*/}"
  done)"
  LANDED_SEEDS="$(list_landed_seeds "$entries")"

  TIP=0
  TIP_NAME=""
  while IFS= read -r base; do
    [ -n "$base" ] || continue
    version="$(coach_seed_version "$base")"
    if [ "$version" -gt "$TIP" ]; then
      TIP="$version"
      TIP_NAME="$base"
    fi
  done <<< "$LANDED_SEEDS"

  # Refused, and NOT read as "nothing has been authored yet". The authorship rule
  # below decides by comparing against the tip's body, so a base with no tip has
  # no body to compare with — and treating that as "identical" would make the
  # FIRST seed the one run this script silently declined to generate.
  if [ "$TIP" -eq 0 ]; then
    fatal "[$BASE_REF] carries no coach system-prompt seed under [$SCHEMA_REL/], so there is no version to follow; the names it does carry are [$(printf '%s' "$entries" | tr '\n' ' ')], and a label like [v0] or [v08] is rejected on purpose."
  fi

  TIP_VERSION="v$TIP"
  TIP_PATH="$SCHEMA_REL/$TIP_NAME"
}

# The label this run owes if it owes one: one past the tip. Pure arithmetic on
# what resolve_catalog_tip read, and split from it because "what does the catalog
# already hold?" and "what would this run add?" are two questions — one of which
# every nothing-authored run answers and then never uses.
resolve_target_version() {
  NEXT=$((TIP + 1))
  TARGET_NAME="seed-coach-system-prompt-v$NEXT.sql"
  TARGET_FILE="$SCHEMA_DIR/$TARGET_NAME"
  TARGET_VERSION="v$NEXT"
}

# This run's own seeds: present on disk, absent from the base.
#
# "Is this seed landed?" is a THREE-valued question — grep matched, grep did not
# match, grep could not read — and `if !` gives it two answers, filing the fault
# under "absent from <base-ref>", which is the branch whose caller runs `rm -f`.
# The status is split here the same way read_pin and drop_order_line already
# split it, because this is the site that DELETES MIGRATIONS.
list_run_owned_seeds() {
  local path base status
  for path in "$SCHEMA_DIR"/*.sql; do
    [ -e "$path" ] || continue
    base="${path##*/}"
    is_coach_seed "$base" || continue
    status=0
    printf '%s\n' "$LANDED_SEEDS" | grep -Fxq "$base" || status=$?
    [ "$status" -le 1 ] ||
      fatal "could not decide whether [$base] is landed in [$BASE_REF]: grep exited [$status], reported above. Nothing has been deleted."
    [ "$status" -eq 0 ] || printf '%s\n' "$base"
  done
  return 0
}

# ── The ORDER contract, read through its owner ─────────────────────────────────
# bin/common is the declared sole owner of what ORDER is, what a legal migration
# filename is, and what counts as a disagreement. This script sources it, so it
# ASKS rather than re-deriving the path, re-implementing membership with
# `grep -Fxq` and carrying its own copy of the grammar — three re-implementations
# that diverged in a way nobody would find by reading: a whitespace- or
# CR-terminated ORDER line reads as absent to `grep -Fxq`, so the append below
# would write a SECOND line for one migration, and bin/db-migrate would refuse a
# corpus this script had just called correct.
#
# Refused up front, beside the other refusals, and never mid-write.
scan_order() { schema_order_scan "$SCHEMA_DIR"; }

assert_order_is_appendable() {
  scan_order
  if schema_order_file_is_corrupt; then
    fatal -s "$EXIT_SCHEMA_ORDER_DRIFT" \
      "[$ORDER_FILE] cannot be appended to safely:"$'\n'"$(schema_order_drift_report)"$'\n'"  Fix it and re-run. \`bin/db-status\` reports the same corpus without refusing it."
  fi
}

# ── Generation ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The emitted file keeps the SHAPE of every coach seed before it: one
# INSERT, the body as `||`-joined single-quoted fragments with '' escaping. What
# changed (RFC 185) is where a fragment ENDS. It used to be a wrap column, so the
# fragment boundary was arithmetic and one edited sentence re-flowed the file
# below it; it is now the SOURCE'S OWN LINE, so the seed's diff is the source's
# diff, line for line — the property this whole line of work has been chasing.
#
# The joiner is `chr(10)` and not a literal newline inside a quoted fragment. A
# literal newline in a diff is indistinguishable from layout, which is exactly
# the lesson 0011:1-7 already recorded about `||` being "layout only"; written as
# chr(10) the one thing a reader must not miss — that this newline is BODY — is
# the one thing on the line.
#
# Read into a variable through a plain heredoc rather than `$(cat << 'AWK' ...)`:
# bash 3.2, which ship-land runs under, cannot parse a heredoc inside a command
# substitution when the heredoc body carries unbalanced quotes — and this one is
# nothing but unbalanced quotes. `read -d ''` stops at EOF and reports 1 for it,
# hence the `|| true` under `set -e`.
IFS='' read -r -d '' SEED_AWK << 'AWK' || true
# The header is printed from BEGIN, not from END, because it depends only on the
# version: the record rules can then STREAM one fragment per line with nothing
# buffered, and the source file is never held in memory as an array.
BEGIN {
  print "-- GENERATED FILE. DO NOT EDIT."
  print "--"
  print "-- Written by bin/prompt-seed (RFC 181, RFC 185) from"
  print "-- prompts/coach-system-prompt.md, which is the file to edit. This seed is"
  print "-- deleted and regenerated at land, after the final rebase, so a hand edit here"
  print "-- is lost without warning; the body-equality test in SystemPromptCatalogTest"
  print "-- is what fails the build for it."
  print "--"
  print "-- The body IS that file, verbatim: one fragment per source line, nothing"
  print "-- trimmed, dropped or re-wrapped. The || is layout, exactly as in every seed"
  print "-- since 0011; the chr(10) between two fragments is BODY -- the source's own"
  print "-- line break, which the model is served."
  print "--"
  print "-- Rollback is COACHING_SYSTEM_PROMPT_VERSION=v" (version - 1) " -- that row is"
  print "-- immutable and stays selectable in the catalog."
  print ""
  print "INSERT INTO system_prompts (name, version, body)"
  print "VALUES ("
  print "  'coach',"
  printf "  'v%s',\n", version
}

{
  escaped = $0
  gsub(/'/, "''", escaped)
  if (first_done) {
    printf "  || chr(10) || '%s'\n", escaped
  } else {
    printf "  '%s'\n", escaped
    first_done = 1
  }
}

END { print ");" }
AWK

generate_seed() { # generate_seed <outfile>
  LC_ALL=C awk -v version="$NEXT" "$SEED_AWK" "$SOURCE_FILE" > "$1" ||
    fatal "could not generate the seed for [$TARGET_NAME] from [$SOURCE_FILE]; awk's own error is above. Nothing has been moved into [$SCHEMA_DIR]."
}


# ── Reading a body back out of a seed ──────────────────────────────────────────
# The inverse of the emitter above: given a seed file, the BODY it inserts.
#
# It is the reason the authorship rule can be stated over bodies rather than over
# files. Two seeds that say the same thing may not share a byte — v25 was emitted
# by RFC 181's generator, which wrapped at column 65 and had no chr(10) at all —
# so the only comparison that answers "did a human author something" is one made
# after both sides are read back to text.
#
# It is a transliteration of `body_of` in bin/prompt-seed-scripts-tests, which is
# the same scanner in Python: two token kinds (a single-quoted SQL string with
# `''` for an embedded quote, and the `chr(10)` joiner), and a NAMED set of
# layout bytes between them — whitespace, the commas, the `||` operators, the
# parentheses and the closing semicolon. Everything else is REFUSED. The harness
# builds its landed fixtures with one dialect and this reads them with the other,
# so a disagreement between the two fails the harness rather than hiding.
#
# The refusal is the load-bearing half, and it is why "everything else is layout"
# was not good enough. Both dialects skipped what they did not recognise, so both
# were blind the SAME way and the cross-check proved nothing about it: a `chr(9)`
# or an uppercase `CHR(10)` vanished silently, and an SQL comment was scanned for
# quotes, so `-- run A's note and run B's note` was spliced into the body with
# status 0. A body this reader mis-reads is a version minted from prose nobody
# authored, or an authored edit that ships nothing.
#
# awk and not python3, deliberately. ship-land invokes this script OUTSIDE the
# Nix dev shell, where the only interpreters guaranteed present are the ones this
# script already uses; a `python3` that is a Command Line Tools stub would turn
# the land gate into an install prompt. Same reason the file must parse under
# bash 3.2.
#
# A seed it cannot read is a FAULT on stderr and a non-zero exit, never an empty
# body — an empty body would compare equal to nothing and read as "a human
# authored something", or worse, equal to another unreadable seed and read as
# "nothing was authored".
IFS='' read -r -d '' BODY_AWK << 'AWK' || true
# The shape this reader evaluates, and the layout it tolerates, stated once as
# named constants: every number below is derived from one of them, so the
# emitter and the reader cannot drift apart through a retyped literal.
BEGIN {
  # The emitter prints these two lines, in this order, and the token positions
  # below depend on that column layout -- so it is VERIFIED rather than assumed.
  HEADER = "INSERT INTO system_prompts (name, version, body)\nVALUES ("
  JOINER = "chr(10)"
  # The name and the version literal, which the emitter prints before the body.
  HEADER_TOKENS = 2
  # The only LAYOUT a generated seed carries between fragments: SPACE, TAB and
  # NEWLINE, the argument commas, the || operators, and the parentheses and
  # semicolon that close the clause. Anything else -- an SQL comment, a second
  # statement, a chr(9), an ON CONFLICT clause -- is a construct this reader does
  # not evaluate, and reading it as layout would answer "a human authored
  # something" for text no author ever wrote. It is refused, loudly, instead of
  # skipped.
  #
  # This is NOT the ASCII whitespace class, and must not be "completed" into one.
  # CR, FF and VT are absent ON PURPOSE: this emitter never writes them, so a CR
  # between fragments means a landed seed was checked out CRLF -- exactly what
  # assert_source_is_usable refuses on the source side -- and tolerating it here
  # would read a corrupted tip as a body and compare it against the author's.
  LAYOUT = " \t\n,|();"
  EXCERPT_WIDTH = 60
}

# The refusal channel. Its stderr is CAPTURED by read_body and re-emitted line by
# line through log-error, so a self-authored refusal reaches the operator with the
# same [ERROR] prefix every other diagnostic in this script carries.
function fail(reason) {
  print reason > "/dev/stderr"
  exit 1
}

# A short, single-line window on the offending input, so a refusal names WHERE
# in a 180-line seed the reader stopped rather than only what it disliked.
function get_excerpt(text, at,   piece) {
  piece = substr(text, at, EXCERPT_WIDTH)
  gsub(/\n/, " ", piece)
  return piece
}

# The SQL expression the seed's VALUES clause carries, once the statement around
# it has been checked: one INSERT, with the three columns in the order the token
# scan below reads them.
function get_value_expression(text,   start, rest) {
  start = index(text, HEADER)
  if (start == 0) {
    fail("no [INSERT INTO system_prompts (name, version, body)] ... [VALUES (] header, so the VALUES columns are not the ones this reader would name; the file begins [" get_excerpt(text, 1) "]")
  }
  rest = substr(text, start + length(HEADER))
  if (index(rest, "INSERT ") > 0) {
    fail("a second INSERT after the row this reader evaluates, at character [" index(rest, "INSERT ") "]: [" get_excerpt(rest, index(rest, "INSERT ")) "]; a coach seed inserts exactly one row")
  }
  return rest
}

# One single-quoted SQL string, read from CURSOR (which sits on its opening
# quote), with '' as an embedded quote. CURSOR is left just past the closing
# quote. awk cannot return a value and an advanced position at once, so the
# position is one named global rather than an out-parameter threaded through
# both readers.
function read_quoted_string(expression, n,   chunk, character, opened) {
  opened = CURSOR
  CURSOR++
  chunk = ""
  while (CURSOR <= n) {
    character = substr(expression, CURSOR, 1)
    if (character == "'") {
      if (substr(expression, CURSOR + 1, 1) != "'") {
        CURSOR++
        return chunk
      }
      chunk = chunk "'"
      CURSOR += 2
      continue
    }
    chunk = chunk character
    CURSOR++
  }
  fail("an unterminated SQL string opening at character [" opened "] of the VALUES expression: [" get_excerpt(expression, opened) "]")
}

# The whole file, slurped into one string. This reader CANNOT stream: the token
# scan needs the VALUES expression whole, so a fragment split across records is
# not a thing it can resume from -- unlike the emitter 160 lines above, which
# buffers nothing.
#
# And it is awk's RECORD semantics, not a byte read: $0 never carries the record
# terminator, so the file's final newline is dropped and what END sees is the
# file's LINES joined by "\n". That is this script's load-bearing rule from the
# header -- the body is the text minus at most one trailing newline -- and it
# holds here by construction rather than by a strip nobody wrote.
{ text = text separator $0; separator = "\n" }

END {
  expression = get_value_expression(text)
  n = length(expression)
  CURSOR = 1
  tokens = 0
  body = ""
  while (CURSOR <= n) {
    character = substr(expression, CURSOR, 1)
    if (character == "'") {
      chunk = read_quoted_string(expression, n)
      tokens++
      # The column layout the two dropped tokens depend on, asserted rather than
      # assumed: a seed whose first literals are not the coach name and a vNN
      # label is one whose BODY starts somewhere else.
      if (tokens == 1 && chunk != "coach") {
        fail("the first VALUES literal is [" chunk "], not the coach prompt name")
      }
      if (tokens == 2 && chunk !~ /^v[1-9][0-9]*$/) {
        fail("the second VALUES literal is [" chunk "], not a vNN catalog label")
      }
      if (tokens > HEADER_TOKENS) body = body chunk
    } else if (substr(expression, CURSOR, length(JOINER)) == JOINER) {
      tokens++
      if (tokens <= HEADER_TOKENS) {
        fail("a [" JOINER "] joiner where the prompt name or the version literal belongs, at character [" CURSOR "] of the VALUES expression")
      }
      body = body "\n"
      CURSOR += length(JOINER)
    } else if (substr(expression, CURSOR, 2) == "--") {
      # NOT skipped as layout: an SQL comment is text this reader cannot
      # separate from body text -- its apostrophes open and close fragments, so
      # `-- run A''s note` reads as a body fragment and is spliced into the body
      # with status 0. This emitter never writes one inside VALUES, so a seed
      # that carries one is not a generated seed.
      fail("an SQL comment inside the VALUES clause, at character [" CURSOR "] of the VALUES expression: [" get_excerpt(expression, CURSOR) "]; this reader cannot separate its text from body text")
    } else if (index(LAYOUT, character) > 0) {
      CURSOR++
    } else {
      fail("an unreadable token at character [" CURSOR "] of the VALUES expression: [" get_excerpt(expression, CURSOR) "]; this is not a seed this generator wrote")
    }
  }

  # The first two tokens are the name and the version literal; the body is
  # everything after them, and a seed with no token past them is not one.
  if (tokens <= HEADER_TOKENS) {
    fail("no body fragments after the name and version")
  }
  printf "%s", body
}
AWK

# awk's stderr is CAPTURED rather than passed through: the reader's own refusals
# are diagnostics of this script, and every diagnostic in bin/ reaches the
# operator through bin/functions' helpers. `2>&1 > "$2"` sends stderr to the
# command substitution and stdout — the body — to the file, in that order.
read_body() { # read_body <seed file> <outfile> <what it is>
  local complaint line status=0
  complaint="$(LC_ALL=C awk "$BODY_AWK" "$1" 2>&1 > "$2")" || status=$?
  [ "$status" -eq 0 ] || {
    # A here-string on an EMPTY complaint is still one empty line, and an awk
    # that died without saying anything has no line to re-emit.
    while IFS= read -r line; do
      [ -n "$line" ] || continue
      log-error "$line"
    done <<< "$complaint"
    fatal "could not read the prompt body out of $3 [$1]: awk exited [$status], for the reason reported above. This script will not guess whether a version is owed from a seed it cannot read."
  }
}

# ── Was anything authored? ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The one question that decides whether this run owes a version at all: does the
# body the author's file produces differ from the body already in the catalog?
#
# Both sides go through read_body, including THIS run's own candidate seed, which
# it would be shorter to skip by joining the source file's lines here. Reading the
# candidate back is what makes the comparison symmetric: the reader is applied to
# both operands, so it cannot report a difference that exists only in the reading.
#
# The tip is read from <base-ref> rather than from the worktree. A coach seed in
# the worktree may be this run's own, and comparing the authored body against a
# body this run just generated would answer "identical" every time.
resolve_authorship() {
  git -C "$REPO_ROOT" show "$BASE_REF:$TIP_PATH" > "$WORK_DIR/tip.sql" ||
    fatal "could not read the catalog tip [$TIP_PATH] out of [$BASE_REF]; git's own error is above. Without it there is no body to compare the authored prompt against."

  read_body "$WORK_DIR/tip.sql" "$WORK_DIR/tip.body" "the catalog tip [$TIP_NAME] in [$BASE_REF]"
  read_body "$CANDIDATE_SEED" "$WORK_DIR/authored.body" "the seed this run generates"

  # cmp's status is THREE-valued: 0 is "the same", 1 is "they differ" — the two
  # verdicts — and 2-or-above is cmp failing to READ a body, which is a fault in
  # the comparison and not an answer about authorship. `if cmp -s` collapses it
  # to two and files the fault under "the bodies differ", so a comparison that
  # never ran mints the unauthored version D6 exists to prevent. The same rule
  # this script already argues for at assert_target_seed_is_generated, where cmp
  # is deliberately run WITHOUT -s.
  local status=0
  cmp "$WORK_DIR/tip.body" "$WORK_DIR/authored.body" > /dev/null || status=$?
  [ "$status" -le 1 ] ||
    fatal "could not compare the authored prompt body against the body of [$TIP_NAME] in [$BASE_REF]: cmp exited [$status], reported above. This script will not mint a version from a comparison that did not run."
  if [ "$status" -eq 0 ]; then AUTHORED=""; else AUTHORED=1; fi
}

# service.conf with the pin line rewritten to <version>. The rest of the file —
# including the append-only comment log above the pin, which is the AUTHOR's to
# write — passes through byte for byte.
generate_pin() { # generate_pin <outfile> <version>
  LC_ALL=C awk -v want="$2" -v re="$PIN_LINE_RE" '
    $0 ~ re {
      sub(/"v[0-9]+"/, "\"" want "\"")
    }
    { print }
  ' "$PIN_FILE" > "$1" ||
    fatal "could not generate the repinned [$PIN_REL] at [$2]; awk's own error is above. [$PIN_FILE] is unchanged."
}

# Everything this run writes is generated into a temp file FIRST and moved into
# place only once every refusal has been passed, so no failure can leave the tree
# half-written.
#
# Staged INSIDE the schema directory, not in $TMPDIR: every install below is
# then a same-filesystem rename(2), which either happens or does not. A $TMPDIR
# on another filesystem — the usual case on Linux CI — turns each `mv` into
# copy-plus-unlink, which can fail halfway (ENOSPC) AFTER the delete loop has
# already committed, leaving a corpus with neither the old seed nor the new one.
# The leading dot and the `.sql`-less name keep it outside every glob the corpus
# is read with, and the trap owns it, so no scratch file is ever left beside
# ORDER for a later `git add db/schema` to commit.
#
# Created as its own step, and not as a side effect of generating the seed: TWO
# phases write in here — the candidate seed, and the two bodies resolve_authorship
# compares — so the staging area outlives either of them and its lifetime belongs
# to the flow, not to whichever phase happened to need it first.
#
# The repinned service.conf is NOT staged here either: which version this run owes
# — the label it is cutting, or the tip it is staying on because nothing was
# authored — is not known until the bodies have been compared. update_pin
# generates it for the version it is about to install, and only when the pin
# actually has to move.
create_work_dir() {
  WORK_DIR="$(mktemp -d "$SCHEMA_DIR/.prompt-seed.XXXXXX")"
  trap 'rm -rf "$WORK_DIR"' EXIT INT TERM
  CANDIDATE_SEED="$WORK_DIR/seed.sql"
  CANDIDATE_PIN="$WORK_DIR/pin.conf"
}

# ── -n: the enforcement half ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Phase 6 calling this script is prose, and a run that skipped it lands cleanly:
# the seed exists, only its LABEL is wrong. ship-land runs this before the
# fast-forward, so the dry run must answer without writing anything.
# Each assert_* REPORTS its own complaint and returns 1; the verdict collects
# them, so one run names every reason the tree is not current and refuses once.
#
# The two gates below are the mirror of the two writers further down, and each
# pair has TWO names rather than one name and a mode smuggled into its value
# slot. `assert_no_run_owned_seed_but none` read as a filename that happens to be
# spelled `none`, so the destructive reading of the line depended on recalling
# that no real seed can be called that. The mode is in the NAME now, and no
# sentinel exists to type. <reason> is the half that genuinely differs: a stale
# LABEL and a version that must not exist AT ALL are two different things to tell
# an operator about the same file.
#
# The keep-nothing worker is the private one: its `<keep>` is data only, and the
# required-argument discipline lives on the public entry point, where a caller
# that drops the argument must not silently select the wider mode.
report_run_owned_seeds_other_than() { # report_run_owned_seeds_other_than <keep> <reason>
  local keep="$1"
  local reason="$2"
  local base owned stale=0
  # Assigned, not spliced straight into the here-string: `set -e` does not see
  # the status of a command substitution used as a redirection word, so a
  # producer that died halfway would hand this loop a TRUNCATED list of run-owned
  # seeds and the gate would report a clean tree.
  owned="$(list_run_owned_seeds)"
  while IFS= read -r base; do
    [ -n "$base" ] || continue
    if [ -n "$keep" ] && [ "$base" = "$keep" ]; then continue; fi
    log-error "[$SCHEMA_DIR/$base] is this run's own seed, but $reason."
    stale=1
  done <<< "$owned"
  return "$stale"
}

assert_no_run_owned_seed_except() { # assert_no_run_owned_seed_except <keep> <reason it may not be there>
  local keep="${1:?assert_no_run_owned_seed_except requires the seed that may be there}"
  local reason="${2:?assert_no_run_owned_seed_except requires the reason any other seed may not be there}"
  report_run_owned_seeds_other_than "$keep" "$reason"
}

assert_no_run_owned_seed_at_all() { # assert_no_run_owned_seed_at_all <reason none may be there>
  local reason="${1:?assert_no_run_owned_seed_at_all requires the reason no seed may be there}"
  report_run_owned_seeds_other_than "" "$reason"
}

# Only the comparison genuinely depends on the file existing, so only IT is
# chained. `cmp` is run WITHOUT -s: the byte and line where the two part is the
# one fact that separates the two causes this message names, and the remedy it
# prints — regenerate — destroys a hand edit, so an operator has to be able to
# see what they are about to lose without diffing by hand.
assert_target_seed_is_generated() {
  local difference
  if [ ! -f "$TARGET_FILE" ]; then
    log-error "[$TARGET_FILE] is missing: this run has not generated its seed against [$BASE_REF]."
    return 1
  elif ! difference="$(cmp "$TARGET_FILE" "$CANDIDATE_SEED" 2>&1)"; then
    log-error "[$TARGET_FILE] differs from what [$SOURCE_FILE] generates [$difference]: it is hand-edited or stale. Regenerating overwrites any hand edit."
    return 1
  fi
  return 0
}

# Independent of the seed's CONTENT: a correct file ORDER does not declare is
# never applied, and this script's own validator comment says a check that stops
# at the first offender turns a five-line fix into five runs. Chained behind the
# comparison, it was skipped in exactly the case an operator most needs it — the
# one where they are already about to re-run.
assert_target_is_ordered() {
  schema_order_lists "$TARGET_NAME" && return 0
  log-error "[$ORDER_FILE] does not declare [$TARGET_NAME], so the seed would never be applied."
  return 1
}

# The pin is checked here and not left to a Kotlin test, because a Kotlin test
# can only compare the pin against the catalog it can SEE. It cannot know the
# label this run owes, which is the one thing that moves when another run lands
# first — and that is exactly the case ship-land calls this to catch.
#
# <version> is the row this run leaves the runtime serving: the label it cut, or
# the tip it stayed on because nothing was authored. One comparison for both,
# taking the same parameter update_pin takes, so the gate and the writer cannot
# disagree about which row that is; <role> is only how to say it.
assert_pin_names() { # assert_pin_names <version> <what that version is to this run>
  local want="${1:?assert_pin_names requires the version the pin must carry}"
  local role="${2:?assert_pin_names requires what that version is to this run}"
  [ "$PINNED_VERSION" = "$want" ] && return 0
  log-error "[$PIN_FILE] pins systemPromptVersion [$PINNED_VERSION], but the row to serve is $role [$want]: the label and the pin disagree, so the runtime would serve a body this run did not generate."
  return 1
}

# One refusal for both -n paths. They differ in WHAT they check, never in how a
# tree that fails is answered, so the exit code ship-land reads and the remedy an
# operator is given have a single writer.
refuse_stale_tree() { # refuse_stale_tree <what is wrong with the tree>
  local complaint="${1:?refuse_stale_tree requires what is wrong with the tree}"
  fatal -s "$EXIT_SEED_WOULD_CHANGE" "$complaint Re-run: bin/prompt-seed -b $BASE_REF"
}

# The -n half of the nothing-authored path. "Current" here is the tree with NO
# new seed in it: any run-owned seed is a version that must not be cut, and the
# pin belongs on the tip. This exists because ship-land's re-check must be able
# to answer without a seed existing at all — demanding one would refuse every
# land that changed no prose, which is exactly the case the rule makes ordinary.
assert_nothing_authored_tree_is_current() {
  local stale=""
  assert_no_run_owned_seed_at_all \
    "[$SOURCE_FILE] is byte for byte the body of [$TIP_NAME] in [$BASE_REF]: nothing was authored, so no new version may be cut" || stale=1
  assert_pin_names "$TIP_VERSION" "the tip" || stale=1

  [ -z "$stale" ] ||
    refuse_stale_tree "nothing was authored against [$BASE_REF], so the tree must carry no new coach seed."

  log-info "prompt-seed: nothing was authored against [$BASE_REF] — [$SOURCE_FILE] is byte for byte the body of [$TIP_NAME] — and [$PIN_REL] pins [$TIP_VERSION]; nothing to do."
}

assert_tree_is_current() {
  local stale=""
  assert_no_run_owned_seed_except "$TARGET_NAME" \
    "it is not [$TARGET_NAME]: the base moved, so the label is stale" || stale=1
  assert_target_seed_is_generated || stale=1
  assert_target_is_ordered || stale=1
  assert_pin_names "$TARGET_VERSION" "this run's seed" || stale=1

  [ -z "$stale" ] ||
    refuse_stale_tree "the coach prompt seed is not what [$BASE_REF] plus prompts/coach-system-prompt.md generates."

  log-info "prompt-seed: [$TARGET_NAME] is current against [$BASE_REF] and [$PIN_REL] pins it; nothing to do."
}

# ── Write ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Drops <migration filename> from ORDER, or does nothing when ORDER does not
# list it. The filter is staged in $WORK_DIR — never as `ORDER.tmp` beside ORDER,
# which is a scratch file in the operator's corpus that this script's cleanup
# trap does not own — and grep's status decides whether it is installed at all:
# 1 is "the last line was dropped", which is a correct empty ORDER, while 2 and
# above is grep failing to READ the file, and installing THAT output replaces
# the migration manifest with a truncated one under a success log.
drop_order_line() { # drop_order_line <migration filename>
  local base="${1:?drop_order_line requires a migration filename as an argument}"
  local status=0
  schema_order_lists "$base" || return 0
  grep -Fxv "$base" "$ORDER_FILE" > "$WORK_DIR/order" || status=$?
  [ "$status" -le 1 ] ||
    fatal "could not rewrite [$ORDER_FILE] to drop [$base]: grep exited [$status], reported above. [$ORDER_FILE] is unchanged."
  mv "$WORK_DIR/order" "$ORDER_FILE"
}

# Delete-then-write, never edit in place: a seed carrying a stale label must not
# survive the run that supersedes it. The new seed is INSTALLED FIRST, though,
# and the target is skipped here: the corpus then never passes through a state
# with no coach seed in it, so a failure between the two steps cannot leave one
# behind. (It is the same set either way — the installed target is run-owned by
# construction — so the skip is what makes the order safe, not an exception to
# it.)
#
# Two entry points, and no sentinel between them. `delete_superseded_seeds none`
# put a MODE in a slot whose every other value is a filename, so the line that
# deletes every seed this run owns read as the line that keeps one — and the
# difference was a magic word a nine-line comment had to explain. The name
# carries the mode now.
#
# The keep-nothing worker is private and its `<keep>` is data only; the
# required-argument discipline stays on the public entry point, because a caller
# that drops the argument must not thereby delete the seed it had just installed
# and exit 0, reporting success for a corpus with no coach seed in it.
delete_run_owned_seeds_keeping() { # delete_run_owned_seeds_keeping <keep, or empty for none>
  local keep="$1"
  local base owned
  # Assigned, not spliced straight into the here-string below: `set -e` does not
  # see the status of a command substitution used as a redirection word, so a
  # producer that died halfway would hand this loop a TRUNCATED list and the run
  # would delete part of what it owns and report success.
  owned="$(list_run_owned_seeds)"
  while IFS= read -r base; do
    [ -n "$base" ] || continue
    if [ -n "$keep" ] && [ "$base" = "$keep" ]; then continue; fi
    rm -f "$SCHEMA_DIR/$base"
    drop_order_line "$base"
    log-info "prompt-seed: removed this run's previous seed [$base] (it is not in [$BASE_REF])."
  done <<< "$owned"
}

# Every run-owned seed except <keep>, which is the one just installed.
delete_run_owned_seeds_except() { # delete_run_owned_seeds_except <keep>
  local keep="${1:?delete_run_owned_seeds_except requires the seed to keep}"
  delete_run_owned_seeds_keeping "$keep"
}

# Every run-owned seed, with nothing kept: this run owes no version, so each seed
# it previously generated is churn to be taken back out.
delete_every_run_owned_seed() {
  delete_run_owned_seeds_keeping ""
}

append_target_order_line() {
  # The delete loop above rewrote ORDER, so the scan behind schema_order_lists is
  # stale. Re-read the corpus rather than reasoning about what the loop did.
  scan_order
  schema_order_lists "$TARGET_NAME" && return 0
  printf '%s\n' "$TARGET_NAME" >> "$ORDER_FILE"
  log-info "prompt-seed: appended [$TARGET_NAME] to [$ORDER_FILE]."
}

update_pin() { # update_pin <version>
  local want="${1:?update_pin requires the version to pin as an argument}"
  [ "$PINNED_VERSION" = "$want" ] && return 0
  generate_pin "$CANDIDATE_PIN" "$want"
  mv "$CANDIDATE_PIN" "$PIN_FILE"
  log-info "prompt-seed: repinned [$PIN_REL] systemPromptVersion from [$PINNED_VERSION] to [$want]."
  return 0
}

write_generated_tree() {
  mv "$CANDIDATE_SEED" "$TARGET_FILE"
  delete_run_owned_seeds_except "$TARGET_NAME"
  append_target_order_line
  update_pin "$TARGET_VERSION"
  log-info "prompt-seed: wrote [$TARGET_FILE] from [$SOURCE_FILE], following [v$TIP] in [$BASE_REF]."
}

# Nothing was authored, so this run owes NO version — and the tree has to say so.
# Not quite "do nothing": a run that generated a seed and then had its prompt
# edit reverted, or rebased onto a base that already carries the same body, is
# holding a seed and a pin for a version that must not exist. Taking them back
# out is what makes the rule converge — run it twice, get the same tree — rather
# than leaving the churn it exists to prevent.
#
# Only ever this run's own seeds (absent from <base-ref>, the same inference the
# stale-base refusal protects), and the pin only ever moves back onto the tip,
# which is the row that will be served.
write_nothing_authored_tree() {
  delete_every_run_owned_seed
  update_pin "$TIP_VERSION"
  log-info "prompt-seed: nothing was authored — [$SOURCE_FILE] is byte for byte the body of [$TIP_NAME] in [$BASE_REF] — so no version was cut and [$PIN_REL] stays on [$TIP_VERSION]."
}

# What this run owes the tree, once both questions are answered: did a human
# author anything, and is this a dry run? Four cases, one router, and the detail
# of each case stays behind its own name — so main reads as a list of named steps
# end to end rather than ending in an inlined 2x2 with a mid-function return.
handle_tree() {
  if [ -z "$AUTHORED" ]; then
    if [ -n "$DRY_RUN" ]; then
      assert_nothing_authored_tree_is_current
    else
      write_nothing_authored_tree
    fi
    return 0
  fi

  if [ -n "$DRY_RUN" ]; then
    assert_tree_is_current
  else
    write_generated_tree
  fi
}

# ── Top-level flow ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
main() {
  parse_args "$@"            # getopts; -b/-d non-empty; no positional arguments
  resolve_corpus             # -d -> SCHEMA_DIR, REPO_ROOT, SOURCE_FILE, ORDER_FILE, PIN_FILE
  assert_source_exists       # the authored file is there and is not empty
  assert_source_is_usable    # a carriage return, and a source with no content at all
  read_pin                   # PINNED_VERSION, refusing anything but exactly one pin line
  assert_base_ref_is_usable  # -b names a commit, and is not behind its upstream
  resolve_catalog_tip        # LANDED_SEEDS, TIP, TIP_NAME/VERSION/PATH
  resolve_target_version     # NEXT, TARGET_NAME/FILE/VERSION
  assert_order_is_appendable # scan_order, then refuse a corpus db-migrate would refuse
  create_work_dir              # the staging area, and the trap that owns its lifetime
  generate_seed "$CANDIDATE_SEED" # the seed this run would write, staged
  resolve_authorship         # AUTHORED: does the authored body differ from the tip's?

  handle_tree                # -n asserts the tree; a real run writes it
}

main "$@"
